---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import figProcede from '../../../assets/img/fig-procede-soja.webp';
---

<ArticleLayout slug="soybean-processing-method">
  <p>
    The bean contains in particular proteases (trypsin inhibitors) that
    prevent the beans from being digested, haemagglutinins (lectins) that
    prevent the absorption of nutrients, and allergenic factors that can
    affect milk-fed calves in particular.
  </p>
  <p>
    In order to make the most of the nutritional components of soybean,
    extrusion is the best treatment. It improves protein digestibility by
    11%, lysine by 14%, methionine by 11% and cystine by
    5% compared with the raw bean, and it also lowers the crude amino
    acid content and therefore the undigested protein fraction.
  </p>

  <h2>Technical study</h2>
  <p>
    In order to obtain a product with a reasonable energy contribution, making
    it easier to balance the ration, it is worth partially de-oiling the
    extruded beans. In this way, the fat content falls from 20% to
    around 5.4% in the meal, while recovering a high value-added
    product: soybean oil. In animal feed, the element mainly sought after is
    the protein level: lowering the fat content therefore raises the protein
    content of the meal while recovering a co-product.
  </p>
  <p>
    The conventional process has two phases, as the diagram below shows. In
    order to lower the fat content of the meal further, one solution under
    study consists in adding a special chamber to the extruder barrel to
    recover oil. The fat content at the extruder outlet is then 1 to 2%
    lower, making the work of the press easier, so that it can produce meal
    with a lower fat content.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Image
      src={figProcede}
      alt="Diagram of the two-phase soybean processing method: extrusion then pressing"
      widths={[600, 900]}
      width={900}
      quality={70}
      sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 700px"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>The conventional process, in two phases.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Dry extrusion</h2>
  <p>
    Dry extrusion cooks the beans at a high temperature - around 150&nbsp;°C -
    for a very short time, a few tens of seconds. The heat comes from the
    mechanical energy created by the shear and friction forces inside the
    extruder. In the barrel, the water naturally present in the beans remains
    liquid because of the high pressure (several tens of bars); at the die
    outlet, the beans return to atmospheric pressure, which causes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      instant evaporation of the water, bringing the extruded beans to a
      moisture level of 6%,
    </li>
    <li>
      the destruction of the walls of the cells containing the oil, making the
      work of the press easier,
    </li>
    <li>
      an abrupt drop in temperature, halting all browning reactions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Mechanical pressing</h2>
  <p>
    Extrusion de-oils the beans better than a flaking-cooking-pressing method:
    at the extruder outlet, the cells containing the fats have been destroyed
    and the oil is free. The extruder works as a preparation stage for the
    press, which gives the press a better yield, allows it to work at a higher
    throughput and gives it a lower wear rate. Trials show that the meal
    contains on average 1.3% less oil. Moreover, extrusion followed by
    pressing produces higher yields, of both oil and meal, while incurring
    less loss.
  </p>

  <h3>Mass balance</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="table-data">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Mass balances for 1,000 kilograms of beans, extrusion-pressing versus
        flaking-cooking-pressing
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Per 1,000 kg of beans</th>
          <th scope="col">Meal (kg)</th>
          <th scope="col">Oil (kg)</th>
          <th scope="col">Water lost (kg)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">EP - average oil yield</th>
          <td>788.8</td>
          <td>145.7</td>
          <td>65.6</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">FCP - average oil yield</th>
          <td>774.5</td>
          <td>140.5</td>
          <td>85.2</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <small>EP = extrusion-pressing · FCP = flaking-cooking-pressing.</small>
  </p>
  <p>
    As the table below shows, extrusion-pressing produces meal containing
    close to 45% protein, and divides the antitrypsic factors by 4. In
    addition, the processed product is very stable, allowing storage for
    several months thanks to the destruction of the lipases and the
    lipoxidase, as well as to a low free water content - which avoids adding
    preservatives, the product being affected neither by rancidity, nor by
    oxidation, nor by mould.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition of soybean products</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="table-data">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Comparative composition of the raw bean and of the extruded then
        pressed meal
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col"></th>
          <th scope="col">Moisture (%)</th>
          <th scope="col">Fat (%)</th>
          <th scope="col">Protein (%)</th>
          <th scope="col">Antitrypsic factors (TIU/mg raw)</th>
          <th scope="col">Protein solubility</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Raw bean</th>
          <td>11.8</td>
          <td>19.3</td>
          <td>34.6</td>
          <td>35.5</td>
          <td>92</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Meal</th>
          <td>6.9</td>
          <td>5.4</td>
          <td>44.9</td>
          <td>6.5</td>
          <td>77</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Economic study</h2>
  <p>
    From an economic point of view, it is important to know the cost of this
    treatment per tonne of soybean. Assuming a plant producing
    20,000&nbsp;tonnes per year - that is, an average of 3&nbsp;tonnes per
    hour - the costs are summarised in the table below:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="table-data">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">
        Partial costs of extrusion-pressing treatment in euros per tonne
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Partial costs (€/t)</th>
          <th scope="col">Extrusion-pressing</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Depreciation</th><td>3.60</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Financial charges</th><td>0.50</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Insurance, cash flow</th><td>1.15</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Maintenance</th><td>1.51</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Thermal energy</th><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Electrical energy</th><td>15.30</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Labour</th><td>10.50</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Total (€/t of beans crushed)</th><td>32.56</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <small>Data: interest 2%, electricity €0.05775/kWh.</small>
  </p>
  <p>
    The cost, excluding raw material, of one tonne of soybean extruded then
    pressed comes to less than €35.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
